00:04:01.767 --> 00:04:03.868
Can you explain the play
for anyone who hasn't seen it?

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:05.334
-Yeah, it's a play that was
written 20 years ago,

00:04:05.334 --> 00:04:06.467
and it won the Tony Award

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:07.701
when it was on Broadway
the first time.

00:04:07.701 --> 00:04:09.634
And it's about a baseball
player, like, of, like,

00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:13.267
Derek Jeter-size status
that comes out of the closet,

00:04:13.267 --> 00:04:14.534
and sort of the ripple effect

00:04:14.534 --> 00:04:16.601
that that has on his life
and his teammates.

00:04:16.601 --> 00:04:19.367
And I play his business manager,

00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:20.968
who knows nothing
about baseball

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:22.334
and falls in love
with the game

00:04:22.334 --> 00:04:24.434
because he's representing
this star player.

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:27.000
And it's about homophobia
and toxic masculinity

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.367
and racism,
and it's like, unfortunately,

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:32.400
incredibly still very relevant.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.467
But it's a beautiful play.

00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:34.701
Richard Greenberg
is the playwright,

00:04:34.701 --> 00:04:37.167
and he writes these
amazing arias for this --

00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:39.067
The character I play, Mason,

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:40.367
was originally played
by Denis O'Hare,

00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:41.801
and he won the Tony Award
for the part,

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:42.834
so there's no pressure.

00:04:42.834 --> 00:04:44.834
-No pressure.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:46.067
-But it's a great play.

00:04:46.067 --> 00:04:47.934
-Have you won a Tony?

00:04:47.934 --> 00:04:49.567
